items.question:
this debounce fires on every keystroke instead of once, fix it:

function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}

items.answer:
The bug is that `timer` is never assigned, so `clearTimeout(timer)` always clears `undefined` and every keystroke schedules its own call. Assign the result:

```javascript
function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}
```

Now each call cancels the previous pending timer, so `fn` runs once after the keystrokes stop.

items.question:
fix this, interviewer says the results sometimes come back as all zeros:

func fetchAll(urls []string) []int {
    results := make([]int, len(urls))
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i, u := range urls {
        go func(i int, u string) {
            wg.Add(1)
            defer wg.Done()
            results[i] = fetchStatus(u)
        }(i, u)
    }
    wg.Wait()
    return results
}

items.answer:
The bug is `wg.Add(1)` inside the goroutine, so `wg.Wait()` can return before any goroutine has registered, and the slice stays zero. Move it before the `go` statement.

```go
func fetchAll(urls []string) []int {
	results := make([]int, len(urls))
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i, u := range urls {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int, u string) {
			defer wg.Done()
			results[i] = fetchStatus(u)
		}(i, u)
	}
	wg.Wait()
	return results
}
```

Now every goroutine is counted before it starts, so `Wait` blocks until all writes land.

